1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,550
Mara doesn't move.

2
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:02,774
Neither do you.

3
00:00:02,774 --> 00:00:06,599
Above the sheepfold, the ship fills the whole sky,

4
00:00:06,599 --> 00:00:08,336
and the hilltop has the flat

5
00:00:08,336 --> 00:00:10,891
gray light of a room with the curtains drawn.

6
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:14,052
The single circle glows beside the gap.

7
00:00:14,052 --> 00:00:17,328
Outside the band, the two figures wait.

8
00:00:17,328 --> 00:00:19,509
You wait for Mara to go.

9
00:00:19,996 --> 00:00:21,374
It's what she does.

10
00:00:21,374 --> 00:00:24,133
She called out to strangers on day 75.

11
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:26,795
She crossed the lock gate first.

12
00:00:27,137 --> 00:00:28,703
She wanted to look at the stadium

13
00:00:28,703 --> 00:00:30,693
when all you wanted was to run from it.

14
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:32,763
If anyone was ever going to walk

15
00:00:32,763 --> 00:00:35,943
through a gap just to see what's on the other side,

16
00:00:35,943 --> 00:00:37,640
it was always going to be her.

17
00:00:38,126 --> 00:00:39,636
Instead, she sits down.

18
00:00:39,636 --> 00:00:42,073
Right there on the cold grass,

19
00:00:42,073 --> 00:00:44,917
with her back against the stones, facing the gap.

20
00:00:44,917 --> 00:00:47,827
Then she pats the ground beside her.

21
00:00:47,827 --> 00:00:48,634
You sit.

22
00:00:48,634 --> 00:00:51,266
She takes the roll out of the

23
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:53,487
tin and finds her line from day 88.

24
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:54,250
Two.

25
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:55,649
Whatever happens.

26
00:00:55,649 --> 00:00:57,514
She underlines two again,

27
00:00:57,514 --> 00:01:00,590
so hard the pencil nearly goes through the paper.

28
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:02,294
Then she holds the roll up,

29
00:01:02,294 --> 00:01:03,974
open, facing the gap.

30
00:01:03,974 --> 00:01:06,231
The plain figure looks

31
00:01:06,231 --> 00:01:07,306
at it for a long time.

32
00:01:07,306 --> 00:01:08,194
The striped figure

33
00:01:08,194 --> 00:01:09,756
looks at the plain figure.

34
00:01:10,162 --> 00:01:11,142
Nobody moves.

35
00:01:11,142 --> 00:01:12,333
Not all morning.

36
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:13,963
Not all afternoon.

37
00:01:14,738 --> 00:01:15,211
At dusk,

38
00:01:15,211 --> 00:01:18,592
there's a soft sound, like a page being turned.

39
00:01:18,592 --> 00:01:19,862
The gap is gone.

40
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:22,304
The band runs all the way

41
00:01:22,304 --> 00:01:24,351
around the stones, unbroken.

42
00:01:24,351 --> 00:01:26,833
The single circle fades out.

43
00:01:26,833 --> 00:01:28,968
The two figures turn and walk down the

44
00:01:28,968 --> 00:01:31,974
hill toward the mast, without looking back.

45
00:01:31,974 --> 00:01:34,556
The ship stays exactly where it is.

46
00:01:34,556 --> 00:01:36,954
You've been sealed in before.

47
00:01:36,954 --> 00:01:38,680
This is the first time

48
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,507
you chose to stay inside.

49
00:01:40,507 --> 00:01:41,307
On day 92,

50
00:01:41,307 --> 00:01:44,383
you count everything inside the ring.

51
00:01:44,773 --> 00:01:46,746
It doesn't take long.

52
00:01:46,746 --> 00:01:47,373
One tin.

53
00:01:47,373 --> 00:01:48,570
Seven apples.

54
00:01:49,024 --> 00:01:51,113
The bottom of the honey jar.

55
00:01:51,113 --> 00:01:52,853
Three bottles of water.

56
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:54,154
Six matches.

57
00:01:54,154 --> 00:01:56,495
That's the whole kingdom.

58
00:01:57,527 --> 00:01:58,676
At the foot of the hill,

59
00:01:58,676 --> 00:02:00,896
the spring trickles out of the grass,

60
00:02:00,896 --> 00:02:03,176
close enough to hear on a quiet night.

61
00:02:03,176 --> 00:02:05,879
It's on the other side of the band.

62
00:02:05,879 --> 00:02:06,868
Everything is.

63
00:02:06,868 --> 00:02:08,121
So you make rules,

64
00:02:08,121 --> 00:02:10,831
because rules are cheaper than food.

65
00:02:10,831 --> 00:02:13,260
One bottle a day, shared.

66
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:14,760
One apple each.

67
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:16,862
No fire unless somebody

68
00:02:16,862 --> 00:02:18,951
is shaking too hard to talk.

69
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:21,837
Mara tests the band first, the safe way.

70
00:02:21,837 --> 00:02:24,165
She throws an apple core over it.

71
00:02:24,165 --> 00:02:25,411
It lands in the grass

72
00:02:25,411 --> 00:02:27,600
outside and just lies there.

73
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:29,050
Nothing lights.

74
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:30,409
Nothing comes.

75
00:02:30,409 --> 00:02:33,732
The road lit up for people, not for tins.

76
00:02:33,732 --> 00:02:34,626
Apparently,

77
00:02:34,626 --> 00:02:37,818
the band doesn't care about apple cores either.

78
00:02:37,818 --> 00:02:38,647
Under the ship,

79
00:02:38,647 --> 00:02:40,700
the day never really starts.

80
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,854
There are no shadows to tell the time,

81
00:02:42,854 --> 00:02:45,564
only a dim gray that gets a little darker,

82
00:02:45,564 --> 00:02:47,584
and then a little lighter again.

83
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:49,633
On the second night,

84
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:52,415
Mara asks you something, very quietly.

85
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:53,890
In ninety-two days,

86
00:02:53,890 --> 00:02:58,012
has either of you ever actually touched the light?

87
00:02:58,012 --> 00:02:59,098
You think back.

88
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:00,280
The station.

89
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:01,353
The store.

90
00:03:01,353 --> 00:03:02,978
The hallway at the lock.

91
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:04,773
You stepped around it.

92
00:03:05,211 --> 00:03:06,463
You ran from it.

93
00:03:06,463 --> 00:03:08,083
You watched it close.

94
00:03:08,083 --> 00:03:10,359
You never once put a hand on it.

95
00:03:10,359 --> 00:03:12,189
You write that down,

96
00:03:12,189 --> 00:03:15,013
and it looks even worse on paper.

97
00:03:15,013 --> 00:03:17,867
On day 94, the last bottle is empty.

98
00:03:17,867 --> 00:03:19,330
You shake it anyway,

99
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:22,151
the way you shook the last gas canister.

100
00:03:22,151 --> 00:03:23,268
Same sound.

101
00:03:23,658 --> 00:03:24,135
None.

102
00:03:24,135 --> 00:03:25,723
The spring is maybe

103
00:03:25,723 --> 00:03:27,294
two hundred steps away.

104
00:03:27,909 --> 00:03:30,226
On day 27, you didn't step over the light,

105
00:03:30,226 --> 00:03:31,535
because you didn't know

106
00:03:31,535 --> 00:03:33,892
enough to turn yourself into a test.

107
00:03:33,892 --> 00:03:36,048
You still don't know enough.

108
00:03:36,048 --> 00:03:38,511
You just have less water.

109
00:03:38,511 --> 00:03:41,384
So you kneel at the edge of the band.

110
00:03:41,384 --> 00:03:43,652
Mara grips the back of your scarf,

111
00:03:43,652 --> 00:03:44,981
as if that would help.

112
00:03:44,981 --> 00:03:47,419
You put your hand flat on the light.

113
00:03:47,825 --> 00:03:49,084
It isn't hot.

114
00:03:49,084 --> 00:03:50,433
It isn't cold.

115
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:52,372
It feels like nothing at all,

116
00:03:52,372 --> 00:03:55,074
like pressing your palm against a reflection.

117
00:03:55,609 --> 00:03:57,596
You stand up, and step over.

118
00:03:57,596 --> 00:03:59,197
Nothing stops you.

119
00:03:59,715 --> 00:04:00,814
Nothing sounds.

120
00:04:00,814 --> 00:04:02,693
The ship doesn't move.

121
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:04,411
Then you look down.

122
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:06,498
Where your shoe touched the grass,

123
00:04:06,498 --> 00:04:08,163
there's a footprint.

124
00:04:08,163 --> 00:04:08,877
Glowing.

125
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:10,672
Pale, like the band.

126
00:04:10,672 --> 00:04:12,007
You take another step,

127
00:04:12,007 --> 00:04:13,758
and there's another one.

128
00:04:14,212 --> 00:04:16,728
You don't have time to be properly frightened,

129
00:04:16,728 --> 00:04:17,671
so you run.

130
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:19,682
Down the slope to the spring,

131
00:04:19,682 --> 00:04:21,034
with the bottles and the

132
00:04:21,034 --> 00:04:23,439
kettle banging against your legs.

133
00:04:23,439 --> 00:04:25,467
You fill everything you have.

134
00:04:25,986 --> 00:04:27,709
When you look back up the hill,

135
00:04:27,709 --> 00:04:30,077
a line of glowing footprints runs from the

136
00:04:30,077 --> 00:04:32,288
band straight to where you're standing.

137
00:04:32,288 --> 00:04:35,094
You walk back up on top of your own trail.

138
00:04:35,094 --> 00:04:37,654
It feels polite not to make it any longer.

139
00:04:38,189 --> 00:04:39,185
Inside the ring,

140
00:04:39,185 --> 00:04:41,652
you spend a match to boil the water.

141
00:04:42,284 --> 00:04:42,530
Five.

142
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:46,089
That night, Mara writes in the ledger.

143
00:04:46,089 --> 00:04:47,099
Not a wall.

144
00:04:47,099 --> 00:04:50,234
Underneath, smaller: a page.

145
00:04:50,234 --> 00:04:53,445
After midnight, you hear footsteps.

146
00:04:53,445 --> 00:04:54,764
You look over the wall.

147
00:04:54,764 --> 00:04:57,943
The plain figure is coming up from the spring,

148
00:04:57,943 --> 00:05:00,184
alone, bent over your trail.

149
00:05:00,184 --> 00:05:01,830
At each footprint,

150
00:05:01,830 --> 00:05:04,491
it kneels, and wipes it away with its gloved hand.

151
00:05:04,946 --> 00:05:05,703
One by one.

152
00:05:05,703 --> 00:05:07,282
All the way up the hill.

153
00:05:07,282 --> 00:05:08,989
When it reaches the band,

154
00:05:08,989 --> 00:05:11,136
it looks up, straight at you.

155
00:05:11,136 --> 00:05:12,658
Then it walks away.

156
00:05:12,658 --> 00:05:13,395
On day 95,

157
00:05:13,395 --> 00:05:16,643
the soles of your shoes are still glowing.

158
00:05:16,643 --> 00:05:17,258
Faintly.

159
00:05:17,258 --> 00:05:19,639
You only see it in the dark,

160
00:05:19,639 --> 00:05:20,652
but you see it.

161
00:05:20,652 --> 00:05:22,489
You scrub them on the grass.

162
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:24,353
You scrub them on the stones.

163
00:05:24,353 --> 00:05:26,198
You scrape them with the lid of

164
00:05:26,198 --> 00:05:28,162
the honey jar, which is the most

165
00:05:28,162 --> 00:05:30,475
useful that lid has been all week.

166
00:05:30,929 --> 00:05:32,174
They still glow.

167
00:05:32,174 --> 00:05:33,639
So would every step

168
00:05:33,639 --> 00:05:35,652
you take away from here.

169
00:05:35,652 --> 00:05:36,590
On day 72,

170
00:05:36,590 --> 00:05:40,998
the plain figure stepped between you and the scanner.

171
00:05:40,998 --> 00:05:43,767
Last night, it cleaned up after you.

172
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:46,119
Mara doesn't call it a label anymore.

173
00:05:46,119 --> 00:05:48,326
But it can't follow you around the

174
00:05:48,326 --> 00:05:50,931
countryside all night, wiping the ground.

175
00:05:50,931 --> 00:05:52,351
Not forever.

176
00:05:52,351 --> 00:05:54,555
And not without somebody noticing.

177
00:05:54,555 --> 00:05:56,438
So you look at the map,

178
00:05:56,438 --> 00:05:59,257
for the one rule that has never failed you,

179
00:05:59,257 --> 00:06:01,580
mostly because you've only seen it once.

180
00:06:02,388 --> 00:06:03,088
On day 82,

181
00:06:03,088 --> 00:06:05,804
the bands stopped at the water's edge.

182
00:06:06,178 --> 00:06:07,269
Below the spring,

183
00:06:07,269 --> 00:06:10,617
a stream runs down the far side of the hill.

184
00:06:10,617 --> 00:06:12,071
Away from the mast.

185
00:06:12,071 --> 00:06:14,571
That night, you cross together.

186
00:06:14,571 --> 00:06:16,268
Mara takes your hand.

187
00:06:16,268 --> 00:06:18,325
Neither of you mentions it.

188
00:06:18,325 --> 00:06:20,648
Two lines of glowing footprints run

189
00:06:20,648 --> 00:06:23,284
down the hill, side by side, to the spring.

190
00:06:23,818 --> 00:06:25,911
Then you step into the stream.

191
00:06:25,911 --> 00:06:27,829
The water is so cold

192
00:06:27,829 --> 00:06:29,271
it feels like a noise.

193
00:06:29,271 --> 00:06:30,211
You look down.

194
00:06:30,211 --> 00:06:32,786
The glow on your soles shrinks and fades,

195
00:06:32,786 --> 00:06:34,842
like a match dropped in a puddle.

196
00:06:35,344 --> 00:06:36,165
Behind you,

197
00:06:36,165 --> 00:06:38,628
the trail ends at the water's edge.

198
00:06:38,628 --> 00:06:40,640
You walk downstream all night,

199
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:41,458
in the water,

200
00:06:41,458 --> 00:06:44,760
with the bags held up against your chests.

201
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,241
Once, you look back.

202
00:06:46,241 --> 00:06:47,479
High on the hill,

203
00:06:47,479 --> 00:06:50,027
a pale line sweeps down the slope.

204
00:06:50,529 --> 00:06:52,761
It follows the footprints to the spring.

205
00:06:52,761 --> 00:06:54,543
It reaches the stream.

206
00:06:54,543 --> 00:06:55,984
And it stops.

207
00:06:55,984 --> 00:06:57,115
By day 97,

208
00:06:57,115 --> 00:07:00,305
you've walked the stream for two days.

209
00:07:00,305 --> 00:07:01,282
Your feet stopped

210
00:07:01,282 --> 00:07:03,482
complaining on the first morning.

211
00:07:03,482 --> 00:07:04,196
That's when you

212
00:07:04,196 --> 00:07:05,790
started worrying about them.

213
00:07:06,099 --> 00:07:08,219
You sleep under a fallen tree that

214
00:07:08,219 --> 00:07:11,451
lies across the water, on a ledge of dry roots,

215
00:07:11,451 --> 00:07:13,122
back to back for warmth.

216
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,435
Once, you risk a tiny fire

217
00:07:15,435 --> 00:07:17,887
under the trunk, to boil water.

218
00:07:18,277 --> 00:07:18,940
Four matches.

219
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:21,742
In the morning, you check your soles.

220
00:07:21,742 --> 00:07:22,125
Dark.

221
00:07:22,125 --> 00:07:23,850
You step out onto the bank,

222
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:25,492
and look behind you.

223
00:07:25,833 --> 00:07:27,471
Ordinary footprints.

224
00:07:27,471 --> 00:07:30,414
Brown, muddy, and completely boring.

225
00:07:30,414 --> 00:07:33,565
You've never been so happy to see mud.

226
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:34,895
Far behind you,

227
00:07:34,895 --> 00:07:38,183
the ship still hangs over the hill and the mast.

228
00:07:38,183 --> 00:07:39,419
It hasn't moved.

229
00:07:39,419 --> 00:07:41,610
No can waits on the bank.

230
00:07:42,048 --> 00:07:43,793
No circles light up.

231
00:07:43,793 --> 00:07:47,224
Nothing, anywhere, is waiting for you.

232
00:07:47,224 --> 00:07:49,450
Mara unrolls the ledger to the

233
00:07:49,450 --> 00:07:51,369
end of the plain figure's map.

234
00:07:51,369 --> 00:07:52,173
The station.

235
00:07:52,173 --> 00:07:53,432
The drain,

236
00:07:53,432 --> 00:07:57,303
the store, the hut, the bridge, the boat.

237
00:07:57,303 --> 00:07:58,106
The mast.

238
00:07:58,106 --> 00:08:01,838
Past the mast, she draws a short wavy line,

239
00:08:01,838 --> 00:08:03,691
running off the edge of the paper.

240
00:08:03,691 --> 00:08:06,550
Beside it, she writes: off the map.

241
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:09,154
The food goes in the same ledger,

242
00:08:09,154 --> 00:08:10,373
one line below.

243
00:08:10,373 --> 00:08:12,971
The apples ran out on day 95.

244
00:08:12,971 --> 00:08:14,270
You scraped the honey

245
00:08:14,270 --> 00:08:15,928
jar until it squeaked.

246
00:08:16,254 --> 00:08:18,953
Last night, you shared the last tin,

247
00:08:18,953 --> 00:08:21,792
cold, one spoon each, in turns.

248
00:08:21,792 --> 00:08:24,049
Today, there's nothing.

249
00:08:24,438 --> 00:08:25,400
For ninety-six days,

250
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:28,585
somebody knew exactly where you slept.

251
00:08:28,991 --> 00:08:30,181
Tonight, nobody does.

252
00:08:30,181 --> 00:08:31,824
You've wanted that since the

253
00:08:31,824 --> 00:08:33,866
evening you tried to pay for milk.

254
00:08:33,866 --> 00:08:35,403
You lie awake anyway,

255
00:08:35,403 --> 00:08:36,655
listening to the stream,

256
00:08:36,655 --> 00:08:38,909
waiting for footsteps that don't come.

257
00:08:39,346 --> 00:08:42,228
On day 100, the stream runs into a river.

258
00:08:42,228 --> 00:08:44,435
It's wide, slow, and gray-green,

259
00:08:44,435 --> 00:08:47,909
and it swallows the stream without even noticing.

260
00:08:47,909 --> 00:08:49,756
On the bank stands a stone cottage,

261
00:08:49,756 --> 00:08:51,048
at the end of a garden

262
00:08:51,048 --> 00:08:53,337
nobody has weeded in a long time.

263
00:08:53,337 --> 00:08:55,136
You watch it for an hour,

264
00:08:55,136 --> 00:08:56,832
which is very hard to do when

265
00:08:56,832 --> 00:08:59,048
you haven't eaten in two days.

266
00:08:59,048 --> 00:09:00,124
The door is open.

267
00:09:00,124 --> 00:09:01,619
The cupboards are empty.

268
00:09:01,619 --> 00:09:03,618
Somebody packed in a hurry,

269
00:09:03,618 --> 00:09:04,929
and somebody else came after

270
00:09:04,929 --> 00:09:06,919
them and took whatever was left.

271
00:09:06,919 --> 00:09:08,978
But nobody took the garden.

272
00:09:08,978 --> 00:09:11,489
Mara drops to her knees in the

273
00:09:11,489 --> 00:09:13,655
weeds and starts digging with her hands.

274
00:09:13,655 --> 00:09:14,778
Potatoes.

275
00:09:14,778 --> 00:09:18,659
Small, dirty, and absolutely beautiful.

276
00:09:19,114 --> 00:09:21,453
You dig until your fingers ache.

277
00:09:21,453 --> 00:09:22,904
Thirty-one potatoes.

278
00:09:22,904 --> 00:09:23,656
Six onions.

279
00:09:23,656 --> 00:09:25,686
A row of dark curly leaves

280
00:09:25,686 --> 00:09:27,607
that Mara says is kale,

281
00:09:27,607 --> 00:09:29,966
and that you decide to believe is kale.

282
00:09:30,437 --> 00:09:32,899
In the shed, there's a fishing box.

283
00:09:32,899 --> 00:09:33,378
Hooks,

284
00:09:33,378 --> 00:09:36,009
a reel of line, a few small weights.

285
00:09:36,009 --> 00:09:38,807
And at the bottom, a short metal rod

286
00:09:38,807 --> 00:09:41,401
on a string, with a little steel scraper.

287
00:09:41,984 --> 00:09:44,351
Mara knows what it is before you do.

288
00:09:44,351 --> 00:09:46,284
She scrapes it once, hard.

289
00:09:46,284 --> 00:09:49,078
A shower of sparks jumps onto the floor.

290
00:09:49,533 --> 00:09:50,714
Four matches left.

291
00:09:50,714 --> 00:09:53,163
And as long as that rod lasts,

292
00:09:53,163 --> 00:09:55,328
you'll never need to count them again.

293
00:09:55,328 --> 00:09:57,338
You keep the matches anyway.

294
00:09:57,338 --> 00:09:58,888
Some habits are earned.

295
00:09:58,888 --> 00:09:59,662
That night,

296
00:09:59,662 --> 00:10:03,495
in the cottage fireplace, you bake potatoes in the embers,

297
00:10:03,495 --> 00:10:05,781
and eat them so hot they hurt.

298
00:10:05,781 --> 00:10:08,865
Mara writes the number on the roll.

299
00:10:08,865 --> 00:10:09,191
100.

300
00:10:09,191 --> 00:10:10,104
Out of habit,

301
00:10:10,104 --> 00:10:12,539
she draws a circle around it.

302
00:10:12,539 --> 00:10:13,803
Then she looks at it,

303
00:10:13,803 --> 00:10:15,610
and scribbles the circle out.

304
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:16,625
On day 103,

305
00:10:16,625 --> 00:10:19,665
you find what the garden was hiding.

306
00:10:20,118 --> 00:10:20,960
At the bottom,

307
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:24,234
at the water's edge, there's a low wooden boathouse.

308
00:10:24,847 --> 00:10:28,054
Inside, upside down on two trestles,

309
00:10:28,054 --> 00:10:30,203
is a small rowing boat.

310
00:10:30,203 --> 00:10:30,919
Two oars.

311
00:10:30,919 --> 00:10:32,627
A folded tarp.

312
00:10:32,627 --> 00:10:34,436
You turn it over together.

313
00:10:34,436 --> 00:10:36,498
It's heavy, it's old,

314
00:10:36,498 --> 00:10:40,573
and it doesn't leak, which you check by sitting in it

315
00:10:40,573 --> 00:10:43,023
on the river for five very tense minutes.

316
00:10:43,686 --> 00:10:45,781
Then you have the argument.

317
00:10:45,781 --> 00:10:47,797
The cottage has walls,

318
00:10:47,797 --> 00:10:51,265
a fireplace, and a garden full of food.

319
00:10:51,265 --> 00:10:52,829
Staying seems obvious.

320
00:10:52,829 --> 00:10:55,033
You've thought that before.

321
00:10:55,407 --> 00:10:56,487
At the station.

322
00:10:56,487 --> 00:10:57,432
At the store.

323
00:10:57,432 --> 00:10:59,015
On the narrowboat.

324
00:10:59,438 --> 00:11:00,266
Every time,

325
00:11:00,266 --> 00:11:02,541
somebody drew a line around you.

326
00:11:02,541 --> 00:11:05,334
But a line has to be drawn on something.

327
00:11:05,334 --> 00:11:07,029
Mara points at the river.

328
00:11:07,548 --> 00:11:09,407
Nobody has ever drawn on that.

329
00:11:09,407 --> 00:11:11,027
So you load the boat.

330
00:11:11,482 --> 00:11:13,782
The potatoes go in a sack.

331
00:11:13,782 --> 00:11:14,849
Twenty-four left.

332
00:11:15,175 --> 00:11:15,842
The onions,

333
00:11:15,842 --> 00:11:19,051
the kale, the kettle, the fishing box, the spark rod.

334
00:11:19,051 --> 00:11:20,910
The tin goes in last,

335
00:11:20,910 --> 00:11:24,492
wrapped in the tarp, because if anything ends up

336
00:11:24,492 --> 00:11:26,908
in the river, it won't be the ledger.

337
00:11:26,908 --> 00:11:28,877
You push off at dusk.

338
00:11:29,603 --> 00:11:30,687
Mara rows first.

339
00:11:30,687 --> 00:11:32,835
She rows in a straight line.

340
00:11:33,465 --> 00:11:34,674
When it's your turn,

341
00:11:34,674 --> 00:11:37,162
you row in a large, confident circle.

342
00:11:37,615 --> 00:11:39,125
She lets you finish it.

343
00:11:39,125 --> 00:11:41,397
Then she takes the oars back,

344
00:11:41,397 --> 00:11:43,973
and nobody mentions it again.

345
00:11:43,973 --> 00:11:45,484
On day 106,

346
00:11:45,484 --> 00:11:47,555
the river takes you through a town.

347
00:11:47,555 --> 00:11:49,822
You travel only at night now.

348
00:11:49,822 --> 00:11:51,467
By day, you tie up under

349
00:11:51,467 --> 00:11:53,422
willows whose branches hang

350
00:11:53,422 --> 00:11:55,963
down to the water like curtains.

351
00:11:55,963 --> 00:11:58,456
Tonight, there's no way around.

352
00:11:58,456 --> 00:12:01,193
Along the riverside road,

353
00:12:01,193 --> 00:12:03,138
units stand at intervals.

354
00:12:03,138 --> 00:12:05,332
Their joined circles glow,

355
00:12:05,332 --> 00:12:09,734
pair after pair, all the way to a park by the water.

356
00:12:09,734 --> 00:12:10,390
In the park,

357
00:12:10,390 --> 00:12:12,886
there's a ring of pale light on the grass.

358
00:12:12,886 --> 00:12:14,527
People stand inside it,

359
00:12:14,527 --> 00:12:16,001
two by two, waiting.

360
00:12:16,001 --> 00:12:17,439
A small craft settles in

361
00:12:17,439 --> 00:12:19,596
the middle without a sound.

362
00:12:19,596 --> 00:12:21,349
You stop rowing and lie flat in

363
00:12:21,349 --> 00:12:22,663
the bottom of the boat,

364
00:12:22,663 --> 00:12:25,819
under the tarp, and let the current carry you.

365
00:12:25,819 --> 00:12:27,630
Through a gap at the edge,

366
00:12:27,630 --> 00:12:30,061
you watch the pairs walk to the craft.

367
00:12:30,061 --> 00:12:31,545
You watch it lift.

368
00:12:31,952 --> 00:12:33,361
Then a second craft

369
00:12:33,361 --> 00:12:35,172
drifts out over the river.

370
00:12:35,172 --> 00:12:37,943
Its pale line sweeps across the water.

371
00:12:37,943 --> 00:12:39,697
Across the reeds.

372
00:12:39,697 --> 00:12:41,196
Across the boat.

373
00:12:41,196 --> 00:12:42,694
It stops on you.

374
00:12:42,694 --> 00:12:43,964
Six seconds.

375
00:12:44,403 --> 00:12:46,025
You count them with your face

376
00:12:46,025 --> 00:12:47,647
pressed into the potato sack.

377
00:12:47,647 --> 00:12:50,404
Then it moves to Mara, and stops again.

378
00:12:50,404 --> 00:12:51,993
Then it moves on.

379
00:12:52,431 --> 00:12:54,795
The craft drifts away over the rooftops.

380
00:12:54,795 --> 00:12:56,461
Nothing comes down.

381
00:12:56,461 --> 00:12:57,796
Nobody draws a line.

382
00:12:57,796 --> 00:13:00,747
The current carries you out of the town,

383
00:13:00,747 --> 00:13:02,398
and the lights fall behind you,

384
00:13:02,398 --> 00:13:03,730
one pair at a time.

385
00:13:04,313 --> 00:13:05,077
On day 72,

386
00:13:05,077 --> 00:13:08,103
you learned the scanner can see you.

387
00:13:08,103 --> 00:13:10,325
Tonight, you learned it can see you,

388
00:13:10,325 --> 00:13:12,382
and leave you where you are.

389
00:13:12,382 --> 00:13:14,051
You write it down.

390
00:13:14,051 --> 00:13:14,878
Seen.

391
00:13:15,494 --> 00:13:16,614
Not taken.

392
00:13:16,614 --> 00:13:18,649
Mara adds one word.

393
00:13:18,649 --> 00:13:19,458
Yet.

394
00:13:20,057 --> 00:13:20,871
On day 109,

395
00:13:20,871 --> 00:13:23,244
something knocks against the boat.

396
00:13:23,244 --> 00:13:25,677
You're tied up under a stone road bridge,

397
00:13:25,677 --> 00:13:27,524
half asleep under the tarp.

398
00:13:28,107 --> 00:13:28,665
Knock.

399
00:13:28,665 --> 00:13:29,223
Knock.

400
00:13:29,223 --> 00:13:32,446
Something hollow, bumping the side.

401
00:13:32,446 --> 00:13:34,179
You lift the edge of the tarp.

402
00:13:34,179 --> 00:13:35,186
A red fuel can is

403
00:13:35,186 --> 00:13:37,519
floating against the hull.

404
00:13:37,519 --> 00:13:38,225
Cap on.

405
00:13:38,225 --> 00:13:38,843
Empty.

406
00:13:38,843 --> 00:13:41,849
You know before you see the tape.

407
00:13:41,849 --> 00:13:42,801
Around the handle,

408
00:13:42,801 --> 00:13:45,066
there's the strip you wound on day 61,

409
00:13:45,066 --> 00:13:47,612
with your tiny pencil number.

410
00:13:47,612 --> 00:13:48,251
61.

411
00:13:48,705 --> 00:13:50,670
Then you see the second strip.

412
00:13:50,670 --> 00:13:52,007
Below yours,

413
00:13:52,007 --> 00:13:55,113
somebody has wound another piece of tape around the handle.

414
00:13:55,758 --> 00:13:56,733
On it, in thin,

415
00:13:56,733 --> 00:14:02,298
perfect lines, much neater than yours, there's another number.

416
00:14:02,298 --> 00:14:02,717
91.

417
00:14:02,717 --> 00:14:05,110
The day you sat down in the sheepfold,

418
00:14:05,110 --> 00:14:07,050
and didn't walk through the gap.

419
00:14:07,473 --> 00:14:08,992
Mara lifts it out of the

420
00:14:08,992 --> 00:14:10,997
water and holds it on her knees.

421
00:14:10,997 --> 00:14:12,297
For forty-eight days,

422
00:14:12,297 --> 00:14:16,670
this can has turned up on rails, towpaths, and lock gates.

423
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:18,717
This time, it came by water.

424
00:14:18,717 --> 00:14:21,793
The one road their bands can't draw on.

425
00:14:21,793 --> 00:14:23,599
Somebody put it in the river,

426
00:14:23,599 --> 00:14:26,565
upstream, and let the current do the rest.

427
00:14:26,565 --> 00:14:28,945
Somebody who knew you'd be on the river.

428
00:14:29,559 --> 00:14:31,921
Mara turns it slowly in her hands.

429
00:14:31,921 --> 00:14:34,616
She says it isn't following you anymore.

430
00:14:34,616 --> 00:14:35,938
It's writing to you.

431
00:14:35,938 --> 00:14:37,033
You don't argue.

432
00:14:37,033 --> 00:14:38,270
It signed your can.

433
00:14:38,270 --> 00:14:40,364
That's practically a letter.

434
00:14:40,770 --> 00:14:43,388
This time, you don't leave it behind.

435
00:14:43,388 --> 00:14:45,725
Mara ties it to the back of the

436
00:14:45,725 --> 00:14:47,378
boat with a length of fishing line.

437
00:14:47,378 --> 00:14:50,743
If it wants to follow you, it can ride.

438
00:14:50,743 --> 00:14:53,720
On day 112, you wake up in a white boat.

439
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:55,138
Frost covers the tarp,

440
00:14:55,138 --> 00:14:56,838
the oars, and the can.

441
00:14:56,838 --> 00:14:58,958
Your breath hangs over the water.

442
00:14:58,958 --> 00:15:00,975
Your fingers don't want to close

443
00:15:00,975 --> 00:15:01,960
around the oars,

444
00:15:01,960 --> 00:15:04,804
and once they do, they don't want to open again.

445
00:15:04,804 --> 00:15:06,642
You count the food in the cold,

446
00:15:06,642 --> 00:15:09,494
which somehow makes the numbers smaller.

447
00:15:09,494 --> 00:15:10,749
Fifteen potatoes.

448
00:15:11,108 --> 00:15:11,986
Two onions.

449
00:15:11,986 --> 00:15:13,865
The kale is gone.

450
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:15,705
Every evening for a week,

451
00:15:15,705 --> 00:15:18,573
Mara has dropped the fishing line over the side.

452
00:15:18,573 --> 00:15:20,098
Every evening for a week,

453
00:15:20,098 --> 00:15:22,197
the river has ignored her.

454
00:15:22,197 --> 00:15:23,927
Tonight, the line twitches.

455
00:15:23,927 --> 00:15:25,162
She pulls up a fish

456
00:15:25,162 --> 00:15:26,996
about the length of her hand.

457
00:15:26,996 --> 00:15:28,822
She holds it up like a trophy,

458
00:15:28,822 --> 00:15:30,784
and honestly, it is one.

459
00:15:30,784 --> 00:15:32,383
You cook it on the bank,

460
00:15:32,383 --> 00:15:34,945
in a hollow, with one shower of sparks,

461
00:15:34,945 --> 00:15:37,231
and share it down to the bones.

462
00:15:37,231 --> 00:15:38,450
It's the first fresh thing

463
00:15:38,450 --> 00:15:40,429
you've eaten since the apples.

464
00:15:40,429 --> 00:15:42,710
But you both know what the frost means.

465
00:15:42,710 --> 00:15:44,392
The nights will get longer.

466
00:15:44,392 --> 00:15:45,808
The boat has no walls,

467
00:15:45,808 --> 00:15:47,648
and a tarp isn't a roof.

468
00:15:48,054 --> 00:15:50,288
You need somewhere to spend the winter.

469
00:15:50,288 --> 00:15:50,958
That night,

470
00:15:50,958 --> 00:15:53,799
rowing, you hear it before you see it.

471
00:15:53,799 --> 00:15:55,142
A steady roar,

472
00:15:55,142 --> 00:15:57,415
somewhere ahead in the dark.

473
00:15:57,415 --> 00:15:58,042
A weir.

474
00:15:58,042 --> 00:16:01,149
The whole river pours over a long,

475
00:16:01,149 --> 00:16:02,502
low step of stone.

476
00:16:02,502 --> 00:16:05,269
Beside it, on a narrow island between

477
00:16:05,269 --> 00:16:06,961
the river and a rushing channel,

478
00:16:06,961 --> 00:16:09,699
stands a tall stone building with a big wooden

479
00:16:09,699 --> 00:16:12,880
wheel on its side, turning slowly in the dark.

480
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:14,019
A water mill.

481
00:16:14,019 --> 00:16:16,420
A narrow walkway runs out to

482
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:18,306
it along the top of the weir.

483
00:16:18,306 --> 00:16:21,009
Every other side of it is water.

484
00:16:21,009 --> 00:16:21,917
On day 116,

485
00:16:21,917 --> 00:16:24,589
the mill has been yours for four days.

486
00:16:24,589 --> 00:16:27,421
The ground floor is all wood and stone.

487
00:16:27,876 --> 00:16:29,598
Two huge round millstones,

488
00:16:29,598 --> 00:16:32,034
wooden gears taller than you,

489
00:16:32,034 --> 00:16:35,079
and flour dust on everything, like old snow.

490
00:16:35,566 --> 00:16:37,077
Upstairs, there's a loft,

491
00:16:37,077 --> 00:16:39,662
a fireplace, and a little shop counter,

492
00:16:39,662 --> 00:16:40,886
where people once bought a

493
00:16:40,886 --> 00:16:42,773
bag of something on their way out.

494
00:16:43,277 --> 00:16:44,275
Under the counter,

495
00:16:44,275 --> 00:16:46,956
there are paper bags of oats and flour.

496
00:16:46,956 --> 00:16:48,373
Mice found them first.

497
00:16:48,373 --> 00:16:50,104
But mice are small,

498
00:16:50,104 --> 00:16:52,156
and they don't eat much.

499
00:16:52,156 --> 00:16:53,470
Nine bags of oats.

500
00:16:53,470 --> 00:16:54,737
Four of flour.

501
00:16:54,737 --> 00:16:55,909
You count them three times,

502
00:16:55,909 --> 00:16:58,563
because you don't believe it the first two.

503
00:16:58,563 --> 00:17:01,070
Mara makes porridge with boiled river water,

504
00:17:01,070 --> 00:17:02,573
and it's the best

505
00:17:02,573 --> 00:17:04,222
thing either of you has ever tasted.

506
00:17:04,222 --> 00:17:06,432
You hide the boat in the shadow of the wheel,

507
00:17:06,432 --> 00:17:08,078
out of sight from both banks.

508
00:17:08,078 --> 00:17:10,024
Then you look at the walkway.

509
00:17:10,333 --> 00:17:12,968
It's the only way onto the island on foot.

510
00:17:12,968 --> 00:17:13,859
Planks,

511
00:17:13,859 --> 00:17:17,150
laid on iron brackets along the top of the weir.

512
00:17:17,150 --> 00:17:18,385
So every evening,

513
00:17:18,385 --> 00:17:20,405
you lift the last two planks at the

514
00:17:20,405 --> 00:17:22,559
island end and stack them inside.

515
00:17:22,559 --> 00:17:25,090
Every morning, you put them back.

516
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,493
Between them, there's a gap of

517
00:17:27,493 --> 00:17:29,567
rushing white water that nobody,

518
00:17:29,567 --> 00:17:32,089
of any species, would try to cross.

519
00:17:32,089 --> 00:17:33,928
You sleep in the loft,

520
00:17:33,928 --> 00:17:36,281
by the fire, with the wheel creaking below you.

521
00:17:36,281 --> 00:17:38,447
For the first time since the station,

522
00:17:38,447 --> 00:17:40,320
you're staying somewhere because it's safe,

523
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:42,152
not because you're tired.

524
00:17:42,152 --> 00:17:44,243
Mara writes it on the roll.

525
00:17:44,243 --> 00:17:45,130
Island.

526
00:17:45,130 --> 00:17:47,089
She doesn't add for now.

527
00:17:47,089 --> 00:17:48,030
You notice.

528
00:17:48,030 --> 00:17:51,322
On day 120, the planks are still stacked inside,

529
00:17:51,322 --> 00:17:52,465
where you left them.

530
00:17:52,465 --> 00:17:54,117
You come down at first light.

531
00:17:54,117 --> 00:17:56,554
The gap in the walkway is still there,

532
00:17:56,554 --> 00:17:58,415
with white water roaring through it.

533
00:17:58,415 --> 00:18:00,104
And on your side of it,

534
00:18:00,104 --> 00:18:03,225
sitting on the cold stone with frost on its shoulders,

535
00:18:03,225 --> 00:18:05,106
is the plain figure.

536
00:18:05,106 --> 00:18:05,949
It's alone.

537
00:18:05,949 --> 00:18:07,878
No striped figure.

538
00:18:07,878 --> 00:18:08,883
No scanner.

539
00:18:08,883 --> 00:18:11,227
No small craft on the bank.

540
00:18:11,227 --> 00:18:12,896
And no case.

541
00:18:12,896 --> 00:18:14,255
For a hundred and twenty days,

542
00:18:14,255 --> 00:18:18,276
you have never seen it without that flat silver case.

543
00:18:18,276 --> 00:18:21,151
It carried it out of the craft on day one.

544
00:18:21,151 --> 00:18:22,683
It opened it to draw every

545
00:18:22,683 --> 00:18:24,684
band you've ever had to run from.

546
00:18:24,684 --> 00:18:26,910
Now its gloved hands are empty,

547
00:18:26,910 --> 00:18:28,428
resting on its knees.

548
00:18:28,428 --> 00:18:30,360
Its suit is soaked and dark,

549
00:18:30,360 --> 00:18:32,251
all the way up to its chest.

550
00:18:32,251 --> 00:18:34,069
It didn't cross the walkway.

551
00:18:34,069 --> 00:18:35,659
It came through the water.

552
00:18:35,659 --> 00:18:38,259
The one place their light has never gone.

553
00:18:38,939 --> 00:18:40,689
On day 94, you watched it wipe

554
00:18:40,689 --> 00:18:42,568
your footprints off a hillside,

555
00:18:42,568 --> 00:18:44,846
one by one, in the middle of the night.

556
00:18:44,846 --> 00:18:46,280
You never asked

557
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:47,592
yourself what that would cost.

558
00:18:47,592 --> 00:18:48,565
You think you might be

559
00:18:48,565 --> 00:18:50,036
looking at the answer.

560
00:18:50,036 --> 00:18:51,543
It stands up, slowly.

561
00:18:51,543 --> 00:18:53,123
It looks at the door.

562
00:18:53,123 --> 00:18:53,997
At you.

563
00:18:54,532 --> 00:18:55,036
At Mara.

564
00:18:55,036 --> 00:18:56,214
You don't know if it

565
00:18:56,214 --> 00:18:58,027
has come to take you back.

566
00:18:58,369 --> 00:18:59,958
Or if it has come because

567
00:18:59,958 --> 00:19:01,608
it has nowhere else to go.

568
00:19:01,608 --> 00:19:03,867
It walks up to the door of the mill.

569
00:19:03,867 --> 00:19:04,600
And very gently,

570
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,991
the way you'd knock on a door you've never

571
00:19:06,991 --> 00:19:08,739
knocked on before, it knocks.
